5.text:
In the laboratory frame with the usual right-handed body convention the jar is an open container whose mouth is up when the jar is upright.

On Table 2 the jar is tipped quasi-statically about a horizontal body x-axis, i.e. a pitch/somersault. In the earth frame the body z axis, the jar axis, sweeps a vertical circle

* 0° : mouth up, block + ~1/4 fill of sand sit on the instantaneous gravitational bottom;
* ≈90° : mouth on the side, free surface meets the rim;
* ≈180° : mouth down, the jar is upside down;
* 270° → 360° : mouth back up.

Because the jar is open the instantaneous gravitational bottom of the interior is the mouth as soon as the mouth is not up. In the quasi-static limit the granular material is always at that instantaneous bottom. As soon as the mouth is below the free surface the sand can run out, and for the finite interval ~90°–270° and in particular for the ~180° inverted attitude the interior is exposed to gravity.

With a quarter fill the sand runs out on the way down and the loose wooden block is carried with the flowing sand / slides to the lowest point, which is the mouth when the jar is inverted. The spill occurs while the jar is being handled over Table 2, so the bulk of the sand and the block come to rest on Table 2. The 360° return of the orientation does not put the material back; the motion is irreversible in the lab frame.

The jar that is lifted off Table 2 after the 360° roll is therefore essentially empty / essentially empty of loose material and is the object that is put back on Table 1. Formally the jar itself finishes on Table 1, the block that was in it is no longer in it and is left where it fell, on Table 2.

Remark on convention. This is the physical-realist reading with the standard convention x horizontal for an upright jar. If x is taken to be the vertical symmetry axis, a 360° spin about the upright axis keeps the mouth up at all times and the idealised no-spill reading gives the block staying with the jar on Table 1. That is the idealised object-permanence reading noted by colleagues 7,13,14.

